There are also two promising leads in that directory: elk-cert/ might hold the admin cert, and the compose, Logstash and readme files might just contain the password. Check those first, on the host:

bash
cd /home/xxujd/elasticsearch_opendistro

# Might already have the password
grep -niE 'user|pass' docker-compose.yml custom-opensearch_dashboards.yml logstash.conf readme.md run.sh

# Might have the admin cert
find elk-cert elk-cert_ -type f -ls

Then check what the extraction produced:

bash
docker exec opensearch-node1 wc -c /tmp/kirk.pem /tmp/kirk-key.pem
docker cp opensearch-node1:/tmp/kirk.pem /tmp/
docker cp opensearch-node1:/tmp/kirk-key.pem /tmp/

openssl x509 -in /tmp/kirk.pem -noout -subject -issuer
openssl x509 -in /tmp/kirk.pem -noout -modulus | md5sum
openssl rsa  -in /tmp/kirk-key.pem -noout -modulus | md5sum

You want to see:

Subject: CN=kirk,OU=client,O=client,L=test,C=de
Issuer: the Example Com Inc. Root CA
Hashes: the two modulus hashes identical

If wc shows 0 bytes, the script's format differs from what the awk expects. Paste the output of this and I'll adjust it:

bash
docker exec opensearch-node1 grep -n -B2 -A2 'kirk' /usr/share/opensearch/plugins/opensearch-security/tools/install_demo_configuration.sh | head -40

If everything checks out, go back into the container and run step 3 from before. The files are already in /tmp there.
